  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 PM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery this morning indicates ridging over the eastern \nCONUS while a negatively tilted trof pushes into the Northern \nPlains. An ejecting jet max is centered over this CWA. Visible \nsatellite indicates dissipating low clouds. The waffle pattern\nin the clouds suggests some gravity-wave interaction. \n\nLow clouds this morning helped hold overnight temps mostly in the \n40s, just a hypothetical heat burst away from seasonal high \ntemps. Winds remain breezy today, but strongest winds are\nhappening now as the nocturnal boundary layer has eroded and \nthe sfc pressure gradient is tightest. Thankfully, these strongest\nwinds are forecast ahead of and along the cold front progged to\npush into northeast Nebraska around 2:00 PM. This should \npreclude widespread RED FLAG CRITERIA as the lowest RH values \nwill be associated with the approaching drier airmass. RED FLAG\nCRITERIA will mostly be confined to central Nebraska and \neastern South Dakota, but Knox County has been designated as \nsuch. Talking to farmers and experts up north, the driest \nconditions are found there. As harvesting continues, fuels will \ncontinue to disappear and the fire threat will continue to\ndiminish this fall/winter.\n\nAs the jet streak and sfc low continue northeast this afternoon, our \nwinds will slacken. Expect SW 5-10 mph winds by sunset. With \nthe calm air and a clear sky, temps will be cooler tonight. \nExpect lower- 40s to mid-30s.",
